    silently dropping data — a visible "changes aren't being saved" banner. Silent data loss is the worst outcome
    available here, so it's worth the one extra state.
  - [1mTwo tabs open.[0m Out of scope by the single-user decision, and worth being honest that it's a real hole: two tabs will
    each keep their own in-memory state and the last write wins. If that bothers you, a [38;5;153mstorage[39m-event listener is a
    small addition — say so and I'll put it in scope.

  [1mSection 4: testing.[0m Vitest over the pure modules, which is most of the interesting logic. [38;5;153mscheduler[39m gets tested by
  passing synthetic [38;5;153mnow[39m values — no fake timers, no waiting — covering: crossing exactly at the boundary, a week of
  missed ticks collapsing to one notification, a pushed-back due date firing again, and a completed task staying silent.
  [38;5;153mlog[39m gets tested for append-only behavior and for surviving a deleted task. [38;5;153mstore[39m gets tested against an in-memory
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m double, including the quota-failure path. UI components get light rendering tests only — the bell count
  reflecting unread state — since you skipped e2e.

